{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00748 - MONDO_2313",
  "verbalization": "Carbinoxamine is suggested to have a potential role in the treatment of Vernal Conjunctivitis through a chain of indications that link it to Atopic Conjunctivitis, which in turn is associated with other drugs. The evidence shows that Carbinoxamine is indicated for Atopic Conjunctivitis, and this condition is linked to Tromethamine, Levocabastine, and Alcaftadine, all of which are indicated for Vernal Conjunctivitis. This suggests a possible pathway where Carbinoxamine may exert some therapeutic effect through the same mechanisms as these other drugs, potentially by targeting similar biological pathways or receptors involved in allergic inflammation. The strong consistency across the three paths, with similar scores, supports the hypothesis that Carbinoxamine could be repurposed for Vernal Conjunctivitis, though further studies are needed to confirm this relationship.",
